  "explainer": {
    "summary": "Inkling 2 Mini is an open-weights model Thinking Machines Lab released on November 20 under the Apache 2.0 license: a 26.4B-parameter mixture-of-experts model with 3.4B active, built to run on local hardware such as Macs with 32 GB or more.",
    "why_now": "A Hacker News post titled \"Inkling-2-Mini in 5.4 GB Memory\" drew 582 points. That 5.4 GB figure belongs to an independent compressed build, not the official release, whose checkpoint is about 20 GB.",
    "background": [
      "The official model card reports 79.2% on SWE-bench Verified and a context window of up to 1M tokens. Its middle layers run three times with shared weights, which the card says gives 5.4B effective active parameters per token, a likely source of the 5.4 figure getting mixed up.",
      "The 5.38 GB build comes from a third-party GitHub project aimed at 8 GB iPhones. Its own numbers show the cost of squeezing: 87.0% on GSM8K against 96.5% for the full model. Unsloth's quantized files start at 7.76 GB. A Hacker News commenter pointed out that the repo's headline claim of 12 times lower error is about a different model."
    ],
